        <p class="profile-detail-region">City · Sub-Saharan Africa</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Timbuktu</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1100 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founded</span><span class="info-stat-val">According to al-Sadi's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a>, a seasonal camp of the Maghsharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> about 1100 CE, which became a permanent town in the 13th or early 14th century; modern scholars hold that the evidence cannot fix the time or the founders.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">20 kilometres north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a>, at the southern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, in the Tombouctou Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The trans-Saharan trade in salt and gold; the Djinguereber, Sankoré and Sidi Yahya mosques; its scholars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> and Askia Mohammad I; the Moroccan conquest of 1591; the accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a> and René Caillié</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Timbuktu is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>'s Tombouctou Region, with about eighty thousand people.</span></div>
