    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Timbuktu began as a seasonal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> camp near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, and it grew into a trading town and a centre of Islamic learning as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> from 1324 and then of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> from 1468 or 1469.</li><li>A Moroccan army captured the city on 30 May 1591, scholars were arrested, killed or exiled, and Timbuktu lost its standing as trade moved to Atlantic routes and its rulers changed many times until the French took it in 1893.</li><li>Its mosques are World Heritage monuments and its libraries hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> counted in the hundreds of thousands, which were threatened when armed groups held the city in 2012 and 2013.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>During the occupation of the city by armed groups the librarian Abdel Kader Haidara had metal footlockers, each holding up to 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, distributed to nearly 2,500 safe houses in the city, and about 90% of the manuscripts were saved.</p></aside>
    </section>

