    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1100</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a> dates the start of the seasonal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> camp to the end of the fifth century of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">hijra</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1324</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> annexes Timbuktu to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> on his return from pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1353</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visits Timbuktu and Kabara and meets the governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farba">Farba</a> Musa</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1468</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> absorbs Timbuktu, in 1468 or 1469</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1591</span><span class="rf-text">A Moroccan army captures Timbuktu after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tondibi">Battle of Tondibi</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1828</span><span class="rf-text">René Caillié reaches Timbuktu and returns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to claim the Société de Géographie's prize</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1893</span><span class="rf-text">French soldiers under Lieutenant Gaston Boiteux annex the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2012</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> rebels and Islamist groups take Timbuktu, and shrines are destroyed</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan.jpg" alt="Tarikh al-Sudan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%AE_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%88%D8%AF%D8%A7%D9%86.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">إيان, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> settlements have been found near Timbuktu, older than the town's traditional foundation date. Thick layers of sand prevent excavation in the town itself, but an Iron Age tell complex 9 kilometres south-east of it was excavated between 2008 and 2010 by archaeologists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yale-university">Yale University</a> and the Mission Culturelle de Tombouctou. It was first occupied in the 5th century BCE, thrived through the second half of the 1st millennium CE and collapsed in the late 10th or early 11th century. Research as late as 2000 found no remains of the 11th or 12th century within the modern city.</p><p>When Abd al-Sadi wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a> in the 17th century, from oral tradition, he dated the foundation of Timbuktu as a seasonal camp to the end of the fifth century of the hijra, around 1100 CE. He saw the Maghsharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> as the founders, whose summer encampment grew from a temporary settlement into a depot and a meeting place for travellers, and became a permanent town in the 13th or early 14th century. By another reckoning the settlement became permanent early in the 12th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a> wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-sulayman">Mansa Sulayman</a> founded the town in 1213 or 1214. Modern scholars say there is not enough evidence to fix the time or the founders.</p><p>The town grew from trade between the Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a> and the pastoralists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, in an area already part of an exchange network with Djenné-Djenno, Dia and the Méma region by the middle of the 1st millennium CE. The river's importance led to the description of Timbuktu as 'a gift of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>'.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Timbuktu&#8221; cites where it covers Toponymy, Prehistory, Leo Africanus, Shabeni:</p><ul><li>Leo Africanus, The History and Description of Africa (3 Vols) (Hakluyt Society, 1896)</li><li>James Grey Jackson, An Account of Timbuctoo and Housa, Territories in the Interior of Africa By El Hage Abd Salam Shabeeny (Longman, Hurst, Rees, Orme, and Brown, 1820)</li><li>John Hunwick, Timbuktu &amp; the Songhay Empire: Al-Saʻdī's Ta'rīkh Al-sūdān Down to 1613, and Other Contemporary Documents (Koninklijke Brill, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas.jpg" alt="Catalan Atlas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas">Catalan Atlas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Compass_rose_from_Catalan_Atlas_(1375).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cresques Abraham, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The wealth of Timbuktu rested on trade across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. After a shift in routes in the later 14th century the town flourished on the trade in salt, gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves from towns and states including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/begho">Begho</a> of Bonoman and Sijilmassa. In the 14th century it developed as a commercial and religious centre while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walata">Walata</a>, the earlier hub of the trans-Saharan trade, declined, and scholars from Walata settled there. In 1375 Timbuktu appeared on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas">Catalan Atlas</a>, which shows that it was by then a commercial centre linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and known to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>Under Askia Mohammad I, who ruled from 1493 to 1528, the city enjoyed a golden age, with a central and regional administration that left its commercial centres room to flourish. Merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghadames">Ghadames</a>, Awjilah and many other North African cities gathered there to buy gold and slaves in exchange for salt from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taghaza">Taghaza</a> and North African cloth and horses. Its population grew from about 10,000 by 1300 to about 50,000 in the 1500s, and some estimates put its medieval peak at 100,000.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a>, who visited in 1512, described abundant grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, milk and butter, houses of chalk with thatched roofs and scarce salt, which was carried from Taghaza, about 500 miles away, and which he saw sell at eighty ducats a load. He also described a king with many plates and sceptres of gold and learned men maintained at his cost. Until the second half of the 20th century salt from Taoudenni, 664 kilometres north, came by caravans of several thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, which took three weeks each way.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Timbuktu&#8221; cites where it covers Salt trade, Tourism:</p><ul><li>Horace Miner, The Primitive City of Timbuctoo (Princeton University Press, 1953)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mansa-musa.jpg" alt="Mansa Musa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catalan_Atlas_BNF_Sheet_6_Mansa_Musa_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">attributed to Abraham Cresques, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Timbuktu began as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> settlement and was annexed peacefully by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> in 1324 on his return from the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, so becoming part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>. He ordered a royal palace built there. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in 1353 the governor was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farba">Farba</a> Musa, and most inhabitants were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massufa">Massufa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>. In the first half of the 15th century, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> weakened, the town became relatively autonomous under Tuareg influence and a leader called Akil Akamalwa.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> absorbed Timbuktu in 1468 or 1469. It was led in turn by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-ali">Sunni Ali</a> Ber (1468–1492), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-baru">Sunni Baru</a> (1492–1493) and Askia Mohammad I (1493–1528), and the Askia dynasty held power until 1591. Sunni Ali Ber was in severe conflict with the city after its conquest.</p><p>On 30 May 1591, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tondibi">Battle of Tondibi</a>, an expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Arma</a>, sent by the Saadi ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, Ahmad I al-Mansur, and led by the Spanish Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judar-pasha">Judar Pasha</a>, captured the city in search of gold mines. The Arma became a ruling class and after 1612 were virtually independent of Morocco. Later the Tuareg took control in 1737, Bambara and Kounta occupied or besieged the city, the Massina Empire held it from 1826 until 1865, and then the Toucouleur Empire. The sources conflict on who held it when the French came. On 15 December 1893 a small group of French soldiers under Lieutenant Gaston Boiteux annexed it, and it became part of French Sudan until the Republic of Mali was proclaimed on 22 September 1960.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque.jpg" alt="Djinguereber Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque">Djinguereber Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djingareiber_cour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KaTeznik, CC BY-SA 2.0 fr</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Timbuktu was a world centre of Islamic learning from the 13th to the 17th century, especially under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> and Askia Mohammad I. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> recruited scholars from across the Islamic world, who studied not only Islamic subjects but history, rhetoric, law, science and above all medicine. Literacy and books were symbols of wealth, power and blessing, and the acquisition of books became a main concern of scholars. A lively book trade with other parts of the Islamic world and the emperor's support led to thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>.</p><p>Teaching went on in informal madrasahs, three of which, Djinguereber, Sidi Yahya and Sankoré, are now called the University of Timbuktu and are said to have served 25,000 students. Education was supported by families or lineages, among them the Aqit and Bunu al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">Qadi</a> al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> families. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-baba-al-timbukti">Ahmad Baba</a> al Massufi is regarded as the city's greatest scholar. The three mosques are the city's chief monuments. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque">Djinguereber Mosque</a> was built in 1327 under Mansa Musa, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a> and the Tarikh al-fattash attribute it to him. Qadi al-Aqib had it pulled down in 1570 and rebuilt larger. Sankoré was built in the 14th and 15th centuries, and its libraries housed thousands of manuscripts. The Sidi Yahya Mosque was founded in 1400 and completed in 1440.</p><p>The earliest surviving local documents are the 17th-century chronicles of al-Sadi, the Tarikh al-Sudan, and of Ibn al-Mukhtar, the Tarikh al-fattash. They record the biographies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> and judges and say little of the town's social and economic history.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> absorbed Timbuktu in 1468 and 1469, many scholars left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walata">Walata</a>. In 1591 the Moroccan conquest began a period of economic and intellectual decline, and in 1593 Ahmad I al-Mansur, citing disloyalty, had many of the city's scholars arrested and then killed or exiled, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-baba-al-timbukti">Ahmad Baba</a>, who was forced to move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> and returned, dying in Timbuktu in 1627. Trans-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a>, which carried enslaved Africans including leaders and scholars of Timbuktu, pushed the city aside as a trade and learning centre.</p><p>Droughts in 1973 and 1985 reduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> herders round Timbuktu, and many people left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. After a military coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a> on 21 March 2012, the Tuareg MNLA and Ansar Dine took Timbuktu on 1 April. On 3 April Ansar Dine announced that its version of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> would be enforced, and the mayor said almost all of the city's Christians then fled. The MNLA declared the independence of Azawad on 6 April, but Ansar Dine and AQMI installed sharia. In May and June 2012 Ansar Dine destroyed shrines, including the mausoleum of Sidi Mahmoud, with shovels and pickaxes, calling them idolatry.</p><p>On 25 January 2013 fifteen men entered the restoration rooms of the Ahmed Baba Institute and burned 4,202 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. Most manuscripts survived, because families had hidden them and the librarian Abdel Kader Haidara organised their removal; some 350,000 were taken to safety, and most of them stayed in Bamako for years. French and Malian soldiers retook the city on 28 January 2013. On 8 August 2023 the armed group JNIM blockaded Timbuktu.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth.jpg" alt="Heinrich Barth" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth">Heinrich Barth</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barth_Unterschrift.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a>, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> in 1485, wrote an account of the city in his Descrittione dell'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, published in 1550. In 1780 the Moroccan Shabeni described a very different Timbuktu. These accounts made the city's reputation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> shift from rich to mysterious. In 1788 the African Association was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to find it, and the Société de Géographie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> offered a prize of 9,000 francs in 1824 to the first non-Muslim to reach the town and return with information. Gordon Laing arrived in August 1826 and was killed the next month by local Muslims who feared European intervention. René Caillié arrived in 1828, disguised as a Muslim, and returned to claim the prize. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth">Heinrich Barth</a> reached the city in 1853, and Oskar Lenz and Cristobal Benítez in 1880. The African Association's first explorer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mungo-park">Mungo Park</a>, set out for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and Timbuktu in 1795 and 1805 and died in modern-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> without reporting his findings. The claim of the American sailor Robert Adams to have been there in 1812 is doubted.</p><p>In 1988 three mosques and 16 mausoleums or cemeteries of the old town were inscribed on the World Heritage list. The site was on the List in Danger from 1990 to 2005 and again from 2012.</p><p>Libraries in Timbuktu are said to have held up to 700,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. Timbuktu is now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>'s Tombouctou Region, with about eighty thousand people, fewer than at its medieval peak of an estimated 100,000.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Timbuktu grew from a seasonal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> camp near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> into a trading town and a centre of Islamic learning under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> empires, until a Moroccan army took it in 1591. Its mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> libraries survive, and it is now a city in northern Mali.</em></p>

